## Finding description and impact

The ShareToken contract enforces a hard limit on the number of active vaults to prevent gas limit issues during iteration. This limit is defined by MAX_VAULTS_PER_SHARE_TOKEN = 10.

To manage this limit, the protocol includes an unregisterVault function, allowing the Owner to remove old or unused vaults to free up slots for new assets.

However, the unregisterVault function includes a strict safety check that relies on external state (the vault's token balance):

// ShareTokenUpgradeable.sol
function unregisterVault(address asset) external onlyOwner {
    // ...
    // VULNERABILITY: Strict dependency on external balance
    if (IERC20(asset).balanceOf(vaultAddress) != 0) {
        revert CannotUnregisterVaultAssetBalance();
    }
    // ...
}

This check creates a Critical Denial of Service vector. An attacker can send a negligible amount of tokens (1 wei, "dust") directly to any active Vault address. Since the Vault contract typically lacks a function to sweep underlying assets (to preserve accounting integrity), the balance will never be 0 again.

Impact Analysis
Permanent Resource Exhaustion: Once the protocol reaches 10 registered vaults, it must unregister one to add a new one.

Protocol Paralysis (DoS): An attacker can "dust" all 10 active vaults with 1 wei. When the protocol attempts to rotate assets or add new strategies, unregisterVault will revert.

Expansion Lock: The registerVault function will permanently revert with MaxVaultsExceeded, rendering the ShareToken incapable of business expansion.

Irreversibility: Without a contract upgrade, the Owner has no mechanism to force-remove the corrupted vault or sweep the dust, making the lockout permanent.

Economic ROI
Attack Cost: ~ $0.0000001 (1 wei + gas).

Protocol Loss: Infinite (Total loss of future functionality/expansion).


## Recommended mitigation steps

The check balanceOf(vaultAddress) != 0 is too strict for a permissionless environment.

Recommendation: Modify unregisterVault to allow the Owner to sweep any remaining "dust" to a treasury or simply ignore the balance check if the internal accounting (totalAssets or metrics) confirms the vault is effectively empty.

function unregisterVault(address asset) external onlyOwner {
    // ... checks ...

    // Instead of reverting, just check internal accounting
    // Or allow a "force" parameter to ignore the check
    try IVaultMetrics(vaultAddress).getVaultMetrics() returns (IVaultMetrics.VaultMetrics memory metrics) {
         if (metrics.isActive) revert CannotUnregisterActiveVault();
         // ... internal checks ...
    }

    // REMOVE THIS LINE:
    // if (IERC20(asset).balanceOf(vaultAddress) != 0) revert ...

    // ... remove from mapping ...
}

