## Finding description and impact
The [GTELaunchpadV2Pair::swap()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-08-gte-perps/blob/main/contracts/launchpad/uniswap/GTELaunchpadV2Pair.sol#L232) function first ensures that a fee of **0.3%** is charged on the amountIn (the fee that should be distributed among the liquidity providers), and then calculates and distributes an additional fee towards the `Distributor.sol` contract. However that additional fee is not withheld from the user that made the swap. 
```solidity
    function swap(uint256 amount0Out, uint256 amount1Out, address to, bytes calldata data) external lock {
        if (amount0Out == 0 && amount1Out == 0) revert("UniswapV2: INSUFFICIENT_OUTPUT_AMOUNT");
        (uint112 _reserve0, uint112 _reserve1,) = getReserves(); // gas savings
        if (amount0Out >= _reserve0 || amount1Out >= _reserve1) revert("UniswapV2: INSUFFICIENT_LIQUIDITY");

        uint256 balance0;
        uint256 balance1;
        {
            // scope for _token{0,1}, avoids stack too deep errors
            address _token0 = token0;
            address _token1 = token1;
            if (to == _token0 || to == _token1) revert("UniswapV2: INVALID_TO");
            if (amount0Out > 0) _safeTransfer(_token0, to, amount0Out); // optimistically transfer tokens
            if (amount1Out > 0) _safeTransfer(_token1, to, amount1Out); // optimistically transfer tokens
            if (data.length > 0) IUniswapV2Callee(to).uniswapV2Call(msg.sender, amount0Out, amount1Out, data);
            balance0 = IERC20(_token0).balanceOf(address(this));
            balance1 = IERC20(_token1).balanceOf(address(this));
        }
        uint256 amount0In = balance0 > _reserve0 - amount0Out ? balance0 - (_reserve0 - amount0Out) : 0;
        uint256 amount1In = balance1 > _reserve1 - amount1Out ? balance1 - (_reserve1 - amount1Out) : 0;
        if (amount0In == 0 && amount1In == 0) revert("UniswapV2: INSUFFICIENT_INPUT_AMOUNT");

        {
            // scope for reserve{0,1}Adjusted and launchpadFee{0,1}, avoids stack too deep errors
            uint256 balance0Adjusted = balance0.mul(1000).sub(amount0In.mul(3));
            uint256 balance1Adjusted = balance1.mul(1000).sub(amount1In.mul(3));

            if (balance0Adjusted.mul(balance1Adjusted) < uint256(_reserve0).mul(_reserve1).mul(1000 ** 2)) {
                revert("UniswapV2: K");
            }

            (uint112 launchpadFee0, uint112 launchpadFee1) = launchpadFeeDistributor > address(0)
                && rewardsPoolActive > 0 ? _getLaunchpadFees(amount0In, amount1In) : (uint112(0), uint112(0));

            _update(balance0, balance1, _reserve0, _reserve1, launchpadFee0, launchpadFee1);
        }

        emit Swap(msg.sender, amount0In, amount1In, amount0Out, amount1Out, to);
    }
```

As can be seen from the code snippet above we first check whether **XY >= K** is true, and then we calculate the additional fee that should be distributed to the `Distributor.sol` contract. There is no additional check to see if the **XY >= K** formula is satisfied. There is a separate vulnerability regarding the difference between the current contract balance of the contract and the saved reserve amounts, however if that issue is fixed the issue described in this reports still persists.

 A malicious actor can directly interact with the deployed instance of the `GteLaunchpadV2Pair.sol` contract, and transfer the required tokens to perform a swap where he pays only **0.3%** on the amount in. The protocol team have disclosed in the public Q&A that they intend to use an unmodified instance of the [UniswapV2Router01.sol](https://github.com/Uniswap/v2-periphery/blob/master/contracts/UniswapV2Router01.sol) contract, just with the required changes for it to be compiled with a version of solidity greater than **0.8.0**. If the contract is not modified to account for the additional fee that should be charged (it will be hard to), normal users will also pay only a **0.3%** fee for swaps. This will result in the liquidity providers receiving less fees than they are supposed to.

Additionally the spot price determined by **x = y/x**  will be affected if the additional fee is not charged from the user that performs the swap.  This will intern affect the next swaps and the ratio of tokens in and tokens out. Consider the following scenario:

- The fee accrued to the `Disrtibutor.sol` contract is **0.05%** `reserve0` is **40e18** and `reserve1` is **200_000_000e18**.
- Alice intends to swap **100_000_000e18** of `token1` into `token0`
- If the contract correctly charges **0.35%** fee we get the following output amount for `token0`
**40e18 - (8_000_000_000e18 / 299_650_000e18) = 40e18 - 26.6978e18 ~ 13.3022e18**. The calculated price of x(`token0`) is **299_950_000e18 / 26.6978e18 ~ 11_235_008e18**
- If the contract only charges **0.3%** fee we get the following output amount of `token0` **40e18 - (8_000_000_000e18 / 299_700_000e18) = 40 - 26.6933e18 ~ 13.3067e18**. The calculated price of x(`token0`) is **299_950_000e18 / 26.6933e18 ~ 11_236_902e18**
- As can be seen from the above equations if the protocol charges the correct fee the price of token x in terms of token y is less. This will affect subsequent swaps, and if the tokens are traded on another platform it will create a price discrepancy. 
## Recommended mitigation steps
Consider first calculating the fee that should be distributed to the `Distributo.sol` contract, subtract it from the adjusted balance 
for the corresponding token, and then check whether this if statement passes:

```solidity
if (balance0Adjusted.mul(balance1Adjusted) < uint256(_reserve0).mul(_reserve1).mul(1000 ** 2)) {
                revert("UniswapV2: K");
            }
```

## Finding description and impact


The Oracle extension records price snapshots through hooks that fire on every swap and position update in the Ekubo protocol. These snapshots form the foundation for all time-weighted average price calculations consumed by external protocols. However, the snapshot insertion logic completely ignores Layer 2 sequencer status, which creates a critical vulnerability if Ekubo deploys on any L2 chain.

The README explicitly states Ekubo is a "multi-chain ownerless and permissionless contract" designed for the EVM. There's a reasonable expectation this code will run on chains with centralized sequencers. Yet the oracle has zero awareness of sequencer uptime.

Here's the core snapshot insertion function that gets called on every swap and position update:

```solidity
function maybeInsertSnapshot(PoolId poolId, address token) private {
    unchecked {
        Counts c;
        assembly ("memory-safe") {
            c := sload(token)
        }

        uint32 timePassed = uint32(block.timestamp) - c.lastTimestamp();
        if (timePassed == 0) return;

        uint32 index = c.index();

        // we know count is always g.t. 0 in the places this is called
        Snapshot last;
        assembly ("memory-safe") {
            last := sload(or(shl(32, token), index))
        }

        PoolState state = CORE.poolState(poolId);

        uint128 liquidity = state.liquidity();
        uint256 nonZeroLiquidity;
        assembly ("memory-safe") {
            nonZeroLiquidity := add(liquidity, iszero(liquidity))
        }

        Snapshot snapshot = createSnapshot({
            _timestamp: uint32(block.timestamp),
            _secondsPerLiquidityCumulative: last.secondsPerLiquidityCumulative()
                + uint160(FixedPointMathLib.rawDiv(uint256(timePassed) << 128, nonZeroLiquidity)),
            _tickCumulative: last.tickCumulative() + int64(uint64(timePassed)) * state.tick()
        });

        // ... <store snapshot> ...
    }
}
```

The function calculates `timePassed` using `block.timestamp`, creates cumulative values based on that time delta, and stores the snapshot. On L2s with centralized sequencers, this logic breaks down catastrophically during sequencer downtime.

Attack scenario in detail:

**Phase 1: Sequencer Goes Down**

When an L2 sequencer stops producing blocks, `block.timestamp` freezes. No new transactions are processed, but the real-world clock keeps ticking. This can happen due to:
- Infrastructure failures
- Maintenance windows  
- Malicious attacks on sequencer infrastructure
- Network partitions

During this period, which can last minutes to hours, market prices continue to move on centralized

**Phase 2: Sequencer Restart**

When the sequencer comes back online, it starts processing the backlog of pending transactions. All these transactions execute with the same stale `block.timestamp` from when the sequencer went down. 

But, the oracle sees this as normal activity. The first swap that executes calls `maybeInsertSnapshot()`, which calculates:

```solidity
uint32 timePassed = uint32(block.timestamp) - c.lastTimestamp();
```

If the sequencer was down for an hour but `block.timestamp` only advanced by seconds (because it was frozen), `timePassed` is artificially small. The snapshot gets written with cumulative values that don't reflect the real time gap.

Worse, trades execute at prices that may have been manipulated during the accumulation phase while the sequencer was down. These corrupted prices get locked into the oracle's TWAP history.

**Phase 3: Grace Period Exploitation**

Even after the sequencer is back up and `block.timestamp` starts advancing normally, there's a window where the oracle data is still contaminated. Attackers can:

1. Execute trades during the first few blocks after restart at manipulated prices
2. Have those trades recorded in oracle snapshots 
3. Wait for the TWAP window to incorporate those snapshots
4. Exploit protocols that depend on the now-corrupted TWAP


**Why This Matters More Than View Function Issues**

Tthis vulnerability strikes at the core of how the oracle collects data. The `maybeInsertSnapshot()` function isn't a helper for integrators - it's called directly by the protocol's `beforeSwap` and `beforeUpdatePosition` hooks:

```solidity
function beforeSwap(Locker, PoolKey memory poolKey, SwapParameters params)
    external
    override(BaseExtension, IExtension)
    onlyCore
{
    if (params.amount() != 0) {
        maybeInsertSnapshot(poolKey.toPoolId(), poolKey.token1);
    }
}
```

Every swap that occurs writes a snapshot. If those snapshots are corrupted during sequencer downtime, the corruption is permanent and affects all downstream TWAP consumers forever (or until enough new snapshots cycle through the circular buffer).

**Impact Assessment**

The impact depends on deployment context:
- **On Ethereum mainnet**: No impact (no sequencer)
- **On any L2**: Critical vulnerability enabling:
  - Price manipulation through sequencer downtime windows
  - Permanent corruption of oracle data
  - Exploitation of all protocols depending on the oracle
  - Loss of funds from liquidations/trades based on corrupted prices

Given the protocol is explicitly designed as "multi-chain" and most new deployments target L2s, this is a material risk worth addressing.

## Recommended mitigation steps


Implement sequencer uptime validation:

**1. Add sequencer uptime feed integration:**

```solidity
// Add to Oracle.sol state
address public immutable SEQUENCER_UPTIME_FEED;
uint256 public constant GRACE_PERIOD_TIME = 3600; // 1 hour

constructor(ICore core, address sequencerFeed) BaseExtension(core) {
    SEQUENCER_UPTIME_FEED = sequencerFeed;
}
```

**2. Validate sequencer status before recording snapshots:**

```solidity
function maybeInsertSnapshot(PoolId poolId, address token) private {
    // Skip sequencer check if feed not configured (mainnet deployment)
    if (SEQUENCER_UPTIME_FEED != address(0)) {
        (, int256 answer, uint256 startedAt,,) = 
            AggregatorV3Interface(SEQUENCER_UPTIME_FEED).latestRoundData();

        // Check sequencer is up (answer == 0 means up for Chainlink feeds)
        bool isSequencerUp = answer == 0;
        if (!isSequencerUp) {
            revert SequencerDown();
        }

        // Enforce grace period after sequencer restarts
        // This prevents exploitation during the first blocks after restart
        uint256 timeSinceUp = block.timestamp - startedAt;
        if (timeSinceUp < GRACE_PERIOD_TIME) {
            revert GracePeriodNotOver(timeSinceUp, GRACE_PERIOD_TIME);
        }
    }

    unchecked {
        Counts c;
        assembly ("memory-safe") {
            c := sload(token)
        }

        uint32 timePassed = uint32(block.timestamp) - c.lastTimestamp();
        if (timePassed == 0) return;

        // ... rest of snapshot logic ...
    }
}
```

**3. Add error definitions:**

```solidity
error SequencerDown();
error GracePeriodNotOver(uint256 timeSinceUp, uint256 required);
```

**4. Deployment configuration:**

For mainnet deployments, pass `address(0)` as the sequencer feed to skip checks. For L2 deployments, use the appropriate Chainlink sequencer uptime feed:

- Optimism: 0x371EAD81c9102C9BF4874A9075FFFf170F2Ee389
- Arbitrum: 0xFdB631F5EE196F0ed6FAa767959853A9F217697D  
- Base: 0xBCF85224fc0756B9Fa45aA7892530B47e10b6433

**Alternative approach if Chainlink feeds unavailable:**

If deploying to a chain without Chainlink sequencer feeds, implement a simple heartbeat mechanism:

```solidity
uint256 public lastHeartbeat;
uint256 public constant MAX_TIME_SINCE_HEARTBEAT = 300; // 5 minutes

function updateHeartbeat() external {
    lastHeartbeat = block.timestamp;
}

function maybeInsertSnapshot(PoolId poolId, address token) private {
    // Ensure heartbeat is recent
    if (block.timestamp - lastHeartbeat > MAX_TIME_SINCE_HEARTBEAT) {
        revert StaleHeartbeat();
    }

    // ... rest of logic ...
}
```

This requires an off-chain keeper to call `updateHeartbeat()` regularly, but provides basic protection against stale timestamps.

The critical point is that any L2 deployment needs some mechanism to detect and handle sequencer downtime. The oracle cannot blindly trust `block.timestamp` on chains with centralized sequencers.

