## Finding description and impact

The `Bridge2BurnerGnosis` contract serves a single purpose: to transfer bridged OLAS tokens to Ethereum mainnet for burning. This operation is handled by the `relayToL1Burner` function:

```solidity
    function relayToL1Burner() external virtual {
        // Reentrancy guard
        if (_locked > 1) {
            revert ReentrancyGuard();
        }
        _locked = 2;

        // Get OLAS amount to bridge
        uint256 olasAmount = _getBalance();

        // Approve OLAS for token relayer contract
        IToken(olas).approve(l2TokenRelayer, olasAmount);

        // Relay OLAS to L1 Burner contract
>>      IBridge(l2TokenRelayer).relayTokens(olas, OLAS_BURNER, olasAmount);

        _locked = 1;
    }
```

The `l2TokenRelayer` is set to the Gnosis Omnibridge ([`HomeOmnibridge`](https://gnosisscan.io/address/0xf6A78083ca3e2a662D6dd1703c939c8aCE2e268d#readProxyContract)), which burns the OLAS tokens on the Gnosis chain and releases them on mainnet to be sent to the burner.

However, the `relayTokens` function on the bridge enforces limits on the amount of tokens that can be bridged. Specifically, `relayTokens` internally calls `_relayTokens`, which invokes `bridgeSpecificActionsOnTokenTransfer`, ultimately calling `withinLimit` to enforce constraints:

```solidity
    function bridgeSpecificActionsOnTokenTransfer(
        address _token,
        address _from,
        address _receiver,
        uint256 _value,
        bytes memory _data
    ) internal override {
        // ...

>>      require(withinLimit(_token, _value));
        addTotalSpentPerDay(_token, getCurrentDay(), _value);

        // ...
    }
```

The `withinLimit` function ensures that the transfer amount does not exceed daily or per-transaction thresholds:

```solidity
    function withinLimit(address _token, uint256 _amount) public view returns (bool) {
        uint256 nextLimit = totalSpentPerDay(_token, getCurrentDay()).add(_amount);
        return
            dailyLimit(address(0)) > 0 &&
            dailyLimit(_token) >= nextLimit &&
            _amount <= maxPerTx(_token) &&
            _amount >= minPerTx(_token);
    }
```

If any of these checks fail, the transaction reverts.

This creates a denial-of-service condition: if `Bridge2BurnerGnosis` accumulates enough OLAS tokens to exceed the bridge limits, every call to `relayToL1Burner` will revert, effectively halting the contract’s core functionality.

Since the contract’s only role is to bridge and burn OLAS tokens, this failure renders it non-functional, locking all the OLAS tokens in that contract forever.

## Recommended mitigation steps

Update `relayToL1Burner` to check the bridge's transfer limits prior to calling `relayTokens`. This would prevent the function from attempting an invalid transfer and allow for better handling, such as partial bridging or deferring until limits reset.

## Finding description
*Note: I already submitted this report without knowing that `ServiceRegistry` is out of scope and the report targets both the registry and the manager. So in order not to mark the finding as out of scope while it is still valid, I sacrificed another finding slot and rewrite the finding to make it 100% target the `ServiceManager`*

Neither the `ServiceManager.registerAgents` nor `ServiceRegistry.registerAgents` functions check whether the given instances addresses are already in use as operator address. So if a malicious operator uses another operator's address as an instance address while registering agent instances for a service, it will permanently block the other operator from registering any agent instance for any service in the future.

```javascript
function registerAgents(uint256 serviceId, address[] memory agentInstances, uint32[] memory agentIds)
    external
    payable
    returns (bool success)
{
    if (operatorWhitelist != address(0)) {
        // Check if the operator is whitelisted
        if (!IOperatorWhitelist(operatorWhitelist).isOperatorWhitelisted(serviceId, msg.sender)) {
            revert WrongOperator(serviceId);
        }
    }

    // Record the actual ERC20 bond
    bool isTokenSecured = IServiceTokenUtility(serviceRegistryTokenUtility)
        .registerAgentsTokenDeposit(msg.sender, serviceId, agentIds);

    // Register agent instances in a main ServiceRegistry contract
    if (isTokenSecured) {
        // Get total bond wrapper value
        uint256 totalBond = agentInstances.length * BOND_WRAPPER;
        // Check for msg.value
        if (msg.value != totalBond) {
            revert IncorrectAgentBondingValue(msg.value, totalBond, serviceId);
        }

        // If the service Id is based on the ERC20 token, the provided value to the standard registration is 1
        // multiplied by the number of agent instances
        success = IService(serviceRegistry).registerAgents{value: totalBond}(
            msg.sender, serviceId, agentInstances, agentIds
        );
    } else {
        // Otherwise follow the standard msg.value path
        success = IService(serviceRegistry).registerAgents{value: msg.value}(
            msg.sender, serviceId, agentInstances, agentIds
        );
    }
}
```

An attacker can create a service with many slots just for this purpose. This attack cannot be stopped by pausing the `ServiceManager` as there is not pause check in the `ServiceManager.registerAgents` function.

The operator whitelist too is not helpful in this case as it is per service not global. So if the attacker creates his service, the whitelist won't help. The service can be created without the intention to push it to deploy but only to keep it at `ActiveRegistration` state just to be able to register instances and DOS other operators.

The DOS'ed operators cannot unbond themselves because the service state is not `TerminatedBonded`. Even if the state is `TerminatedBonded`, the operator has not registered agent instance because his address is used as an instance not as an operator. So the unbond call will revert.

The DOS'ed operators can use another address but this will make managing instances harder for the operators. Plus the design supports registration of multiple/unlimited instances per operator for different services so this feature will be broken. Even worse, the malicious operator can keep registering any new operator address to ensure the DOS.

Also, using many addresses by operators (note that this is an unlimited many due to possibility of continuous DOS of the other addresses) will make management very hard for operators. Because the registered address is the receiver of staking rewards and the sender of registration fees. So using many address will lead to fragmentation of the operator funds.

There are currently operators onchain using same address to register agent instances.

## Impact
- Any operator can use any other operator's address as an instance address while registering agent instances for a service. This will permanently block the other operator from registering any agent instance for any service in the future, resulting in a Denial of Service (DoS) attack.
- The feature where an operator can register instances for many services with the same address is broken.

## Mitigation
Add a mapping in `ServiceManager` contract to keep track of all operator addresses so that it will be checked when calling `ServiceManager.registerAgents` if any of the provided instances address is already an operator address. At the end of the function (meaning registration successful), add the operator address to the mapping by mapping it with the number of registered instances. During `ServiceManager.unbond`, reduce the mapped number of registered instances so that when 0, the address can be used as an instance address later.

