## Finding description and impact

The `BuyBackBurner` contract uses a hardcoded path for swaps, which may not be the most optimal for all token pairs. The path is defined as `address[] memory path = new address[](2); path[0] = secondToken; path[1] = olas;`, which always uses the second token as the input and OLAS as the output. This could lead to suboptimal swaps, especially in cases where the pool has high slippage or low liquidity.

```solidity
/// @return olasAmount Obtained OLAS amount.
function _performSwap(address secondToken, uint256 secondTokenAmount, address) internal virtual override returns (uint256 olasAmount) {
    // Approve secondToken for the router
    IERC20(secondToken).approve(router, secondTokenAmount);

    address[] memory path = new address[](2); //@audit
    path[0] = secondToken;
    path[1] = olas;

    // Swap secondToken for OLAS
    uint256[] memory amounts =
        IUniswap(router).swapExactTokensForTokens(secondTokenAmount, 0, path, address(this), block.timestamp);

    // Record OLAS amount
    olasAmount = amounts[1];
}
```
## Recommended mitigation steps

Create an admin function to allow setting the path for swaps.

